# Vallaki's townsfolk about their day (lane 4, F2): Goodwife Marta on her morning round of the market row and the main
# street, Old Kolya hauling festival wicker from the lower town, and Ignat lighting the lamps after dark. They walk
# their routes (the location's `path`) and stop when someone comes up to them.

~ goodwife
if flag.festival_over and flag.vallaki_backed == "neither"
Goodwife Marta [smile]: No festival this week. Nobody's said we may stop smiling, but nobody's checking either. I'd forgotten what my own face does.
-> goodwife_calm
endif
if flag.festival_begun and not flag.festival_over
Goodwife Marta: Off to the square with you. They count the faces, you know. Mine's been counted. I'm only fetching bread.
-> END
endif
Goodwife Marta [smile]: Good morning! It is a good morning. The Baron says so, and the Baron's men are standing right over there, so it is.
* How is the market? -> market
* Heard anything interesting? -> marta_rumor
* [if quest.the_sun_cake == asked and not flag.cake_eggs] The Baron's cook needs eggs for a sun-cake. -> vallaki/the_sun_cake:marta_eggs
* Good morning to you. -> END

# The calm after a council takes the town: Marta still sells the sun-cake's eggs (sq-stuck, finding 122).
~ goodwife_calm
* [if quest.the_sun_cake == asked and not flag.cake_eggs] The Baron's cook needs eggs for a sun-cake. -> vallaki/the_sun_cake:marta_eggs
* Good morning to you. -> END

~ marta_rumor
Goodwife Marta: Interesting? In Vallaki? Blinsky, I suppose. The toymaker. He asks every stranger who comes through the gate if they've been up the hill to the castle.
Goodwife Marta: Not about the count. About a toy. Some jester his old master made up there, that walked about on its own. He wants to know if it still walks. Poor man.
if not quest.toymakers_masterpiece
quest toymakers_masterpiece rumored
endif
interject tag:curious: A toy that walks. In that castle. I'd like to know too.
-> END

~ market
Goodwife Marta: Turnips, mostly. Turnips and Blinsky's toys, which nobody buys, and the Baron's festival bunting, which everybody buys, because it's noticed who doesn't.
interject tag:blunt: So the bunting's a tax.
Goodwife Marta: Hush. It's a celebration.
-> END

~ carter
if flag.festival_over
Old Kolya: No more wicker to haul. My back thanks you, whoever you are. My purse doesn't.
-> END
endif
Old Kolya: Mind the cart. That's a sun, that is. Will be. Every week I bring the wicker up, every week they burn it, every week the sky stays grey.
Old Kolya: Don't tell the Baron I said "grey".
* How long have you hauled the wicker? -> kolya_sun
* Goodbye. -> END

~ lamplighter
if quest.black_roses and quest.black_roses != rumored
-> vallaki/black_roses:ilie
endif
Ignat the Lamplighter: Evening. Every lamp lit by the last bell, or I answer for it. The Baron says a lit street is a happy street.
if guest:ireena
Ignat the Lamplighter: Your friend should keep her hood up. The dark has eyes on this town, and some of them are at the windows.
endif
* Who are the lamps really for? -> lamplighter_ana
* [if flag.roses_rumor] There were black roses on the chandler's step this morning. -> vallaki/black_roses:asks
* Good evening. -> END

~ lamplighter_ana
Ignat the Lamplighter: For my girl, Catalina. Last winter she slept with her window open a crack, for the stove smoke. Three mornings later the watch found her at the lake gate, sitting against the post, smiling, white as milk.
Ignat the Lamplighter [sad]: The Baron called it a chill. The next day there were black roses on our step, and a card in a very fine hand: "With sympathy, from her neighbour across the lake."
interject tag:blunt: He sent flowers. To her father.
Ignat the Lamplighter: He always does. I light every lamp on this street now. It doesn't keep him out. It only means I see whose window he's looking at.
* Have there been roses since? -> vallaki/black_roses:asks
* Goodnight, Ignat. -> END

# --- Evenings at the Blue Water Inn (their `hours`), and the stocks (data/schedule/vallaki.json) ---

~ goodwife_inn
Goodwife Marta [smile]: One cup of Urwin's red, and then home before the lamps are all lit. The Baron likes us in by dark. So does the dark.
-> END

~ carter_inn
Old Kolya: Sit, sit. This is the only chair in Vallaki nobody's ordered me to be cheerful in. I'm not giving it up.
-> END

~ grumbler
Anton the Cooper: Don't look at me like that. I smiled. I smiled the whole festival. Apparently it didn't reach my eyes.
* What happens now? -> grumbler_now
* Are you all right in there? -> anton_stocks
* Chin up. -> END

~ grumbler_now
Anton the Cooper: I stand here till the evening bell, then I go home and practise in the mirror. Third time this month. My wife says I've a face built for coopering, not for joy.
interject tag:kind: For what it's worth, it seems a perfectly good face.
* [if not quest.ribbons] Who told the Baron you weren't smiling? -> grumbler_who
* Chin up. -> END

~ grumbler_who
set ribbons_rumor
quest ribbons rumored
Anton the Cooper: Told? Nobody tells. There's a list. The girl with the ribbons writes down who won't wear one, and the next morning those are the faces in here. Ask her. Don't ask her where I can see; I'm in enough trouble.
-> END

# --- Lane 28 (2026-10-08): the town at work - the market row, the inn's porter, the square and the watch ---

~ herbwife
if flag.festival_over
Old Smaranda: Sage, garlic, wolfsbane. Same as always. The festival's done, so I can stop selling yellow ribbons too. Never liked yellow.
-> END
endif
Old Smaranda [smile]: Sage for sleep, garlic for the door, wolfsbane for the window. And a ribbon, dear, yellow, for your coat. They're watching who wears one.
* Why garlic for the door? -> herbwife_garlic
* Does your wolfsbane work? -> smaranda_parsley
* Just looking. -> END

~ herbwife_garlic
Old Smaranda: Because the Baron says there's nothing to fear, and the Baron is always right. Garlic is two coppers a braid.
-> END

~ cloth_merchant
Master Costel [smile]: Bunting! Festival bunting, by the yard or the bolt! I have sold yellow to every house in Vallaki and three of them twice. Business has never been better, which worries me more than you'd think.
* What else do you keep in stock? -> costel_velvet
* Goodbye. -> END

~ porter
Matei: Mind your feet! Sacks for Urwin, wine for the square, wicker for the sun. Everything in this town goes on my back sooner or later.
Matei: Heavy days are good days. You don't have time to think.
-> END

~ ribbon_girl
if quest.ribbons and quest.ribbons != rumored
-> vallaki/ribbons:daciana
endif
# Ribbons outlasts the festival: she still writes the lists while the quest is open (Storyline QA, 2026-10-12).
if flag.festival_over and quest.ribbons == rumored
Daciana [afraid]: The festival's over, and they still want the list. Who's sour, who's sorry the sun burned. It goes under the lamp all the same.
-> vallaki/ribbons:who
endif
if flag.festival_over
Daciana: No more ribbons. Nobody's counting any more. I don't know what to do with my hands.
-> END
endif
Daciana [smile]: A ribbon for the festival! It's free. It's for the sun. Everybody wears one.
* Take a ribbon. -> ribbon_take
* No, thank you. -> ribbon_refuse
* [if flag.ribbons_rumor] Who wants to know, Daciana? -> vallaki/ribbons:who

~ ribbon_take
Narrator: She ties a yellow ribbon to your sleeve with quick, cold fingers, and the smile goes out of her eyes for a moment, with relief.
-> END

~ ribbon_refuse
Daciana: Oh. That's... I have to write that down. I'm sorry. It's not me who wants to know.
set ribbons_rumor
if not quest.ribbons
quest ribbons rumored
endif
-> END

~ sun_painter
Haralamb: Third coat. The Baron says the sun must be brighter than the last one. The last one was very bright, and look where that got us.
-> END

~ patrol
Watchman Dobre: Keep moving, strangers. And smile. It's not a request. It's a festival.
-> END

~ raker
Gheorghe: Every morning I rake it smooth. Every afternoon the carts churn it up. Every morning I rake it smooth again. The Baron says this is what order looks like.
* What are the lines in the mud? -> gheorghe_pictures
* Goodbye. -> END

~ dog
Narrator: The big dog sniffs your boots for a long moment, decides you are not food and not trouble, and lies down across the square's best patch of sun.
* [if spell:speak_with_animals] Ask the dog what goes on in town at night. -> dog_speaks
* [if not spell:speak_with_animals] [knows:speak_with_animals] (Cast Speak with Animals, and ask the dog what goes on in town at night.) -> dog_cast
* [if spell:speak_with_animals or knows:speak_with_animals] Leave it its sun. -> END
-> END

~ dog_cast
cast speak_with_animals
if check.last
-> dog_speaks
endif
-> END

# Speak with Animals (2024 PHB): a town dog knows the night streets. Milivoj sold St. Andral's bones to Henrik, and
# Henrik keeps the count's vampire spawn in his storeroom (Curse of Strahd, Vallaki).
~ dog_speaks
set vallaki_dog_told
Narrator: The dog rolls onto its back to think about it, and answers upside down.
Narrator: "The digger-man with the spade carried a sack to the box-maker's back door, in the dark, a few nights back. Old bones in it. Old bones that smell like church."
Narrator: "The box-maker's back room smells of the cold dead. More of them every night. They go out when it's dark, and come back before the sun, and they never smell of breathing."
if not flag.bones_theft_known and not quest.bones_of_st_andral
quest bones_of_st_andral rumored
endif
Narrator: "And there's a little ring in the mud by the punishing boards. It tastes of a frightened man. Nobody's come back for it."
reveal stocks_mud
-> END

# --- Flavour (NPC plan batch 3) ---

~ anton_stocks
Anton the Cooper [weary]: Good day to you. I've been locked in since the six o'clock bell, and I have never been so well in my life.
Anton the Cooper: Out there it's ribbons and teeth from dawn till dark, until your jaw aches worse than your back.
Anton the Cooper [smile]: In the stocks, nobody asks you to smile. You're already being punished, so your face is your own business.
Anton the Cooper: Besides, my wife brings cabbage soup at noon and feeds it to me with a wooden spoon. Could be worse.
-> grumbler

~ smaranda_parsley
Old Smaranda: Does the wolfsbane work? It works as well as anything else in Barovia, dear, which is to say you're still breathing.
Old Smaranda: Go on, take a good sniff of the bundle. Don't be shy; I don't charge for the smell.
Old Smaranda [sly]: Parsley? Listen to the scholar. That is genuine mountain wolfsbane that has simply been through a very hard winter.
Old Smaranda: Put it on the sill and shut the latch tight. It's the latch that does most of the work anyway.
* [Insight DC 11] (She is not telling the whole of it.) -> smaranda_honest | herbwife_garlic
* Never mind. -> END

~ smaranda_honest
Old Smaranda [weary]: Keep your voice down, then. Half of it is parsley, yes, because real wolfsbane grows where the wolves are.
Old Smaranda [sly]: It still keeps the beasts off the porch, mind. They smell a window full of soup greens and get as confused as the customers.
Old Smaranda: Here, take the bundle from the bottom of the basket. That's the true root, and I'll charge you the usual price.
-> END

~ gheorghe_pictures
Gheorghe: The Baron says the mud must be raked smooth by eight, so I draw something in it first, a different picture every morning.
Gheorghe: Today's was a three-masted boat, though I have never seen the sea, and then I pulled the tines through and made it street again.
Gheorghe [afraid]: Do not mention the boat to the watch, if it's all the same to you; the Baron has opinions about where people's minds go.
-> raker

~ kolya_sun
Old Kolya [weary]: Fifty years I have hauled the willow up from the river, and fifty years I have watched them bend it into a ball.
Old Kolya: The very first year, I crept into the square three nights early with a tallow stub and set the whole thing blazing.
Old Kolya: The old Baron had the stocks full for a fortnight, but he never found out it was the carter who brought the wood.
Old Kolya [sad]: Look at these knuckles now; I have built two thousand suns since then, and not one of them kept my hands warm.
-> carter

~ costel_velvet
Master Costel [smile]: Yellow linen, yellow wool, yellow ribbon by the bolt; if it looks like butter or brass, the town has bought me clean out of it.
Master Costel [sly]: Of course, a sensible merchant keeps one roll of good, heavy black velvet wrapped in oilcloth behind the cellar stairs.
Master Costel: I am holding that back for afterwards, and no, I will not say after what, though I have the yardstick ready.
-> cloth_merchant
